{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "paragraph", "parent_tag": "01IM", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{O}_X$", "MATH_0002": "$X$", "MATH_0003": "$X$", "MATH_0004": "$U \\subset X$", "MATH_0005": "$\\widetilde M$", "MATH_0006": "$\\mathcal{O}_X(U)$", "MATH_0007": "$M$", "REF_0001": "\\ref{lemma-open-subspace-scheme}", "REF_0002": "\\ref{section-quasi-coherent-affine}", "STRUCT_0001": "\\noindent", "STRUCT_0002": "\\medskip", "STRUCT_0003": "\\noindent"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": ""}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:b09646c6ace3d9b2caa331a10baa283348b949fb20f719a58f44ff7832530031", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8c10dea04efb3c54d96317d3406adf39a7d5165c399f24d120c438a879d74550", "source_text": "\n\n<STRUCT_0001>\nIn Lemma <REF_0001> we saw that any open subspace\nof a scheme is a scheme. Below we will prove that the same holds for\na closed subspace of a scheme.\n\n<STRUCT_0002><STRUCT_0003>\nNote that the notion of a quasi-coherent sheaf of <MATH_0001>-modules\nis defined for any ringed space <MATH_0002> in particular when <MATH_0003> is a scheme.\nBy our efforts in Section <REF_0002> we\nknow that such a sheaf is on any affine open <MATH_0004>\nof the form <MATH_0005> for some <MATH_0006>-module <MATH_0007>.\n\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:eaaa509ffd5bdc5731de91a5467676820fb9b704edc564d2b60185c95f0b5db4", "unit_id": "tag:01IM:prose-0001"}
{"chapter": "schemes", "node_kind": "lemma", "parent_tag": "01IM", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$X$", "MATH_0002": "$i : Z \\to X$", "MATH_0003": "$Z$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{I}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{O}_X \\to i_*\\mathcal{O}_Z$", "MATH_0006": "$U = \\Spec(R)$", "MATH_0007": "$X$", "MATH_0008": "$i^{-1}(U) \\to U$", "MATH_0009": "$\\Spec(R/I) \\to \\Spec(R)$", "MATH_0010": "$I \\subset R$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{I}|_U = \\widetilde I$", "MATH_0012": "$X$", "STRUCT_0001": "\\begin{enumerate}", "STRUCT_0002": "\\item", "STRUCT_0003": "\\item", "STRUCT_0004": "\\item", "STRUCT_0005": "\\item", "STRUCT_0006": "\\end{enumerate}"}, "render": {"prefix": "\\begin{lemma}\n\\label{lemma-closed-subspace-scheme}\n", "suffix": "\\end{lemma}"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:69c1bc89fb1aa9f2fef7ff2ae67beb081dc98a11172b221f2968245bdf86cc37", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:0683c11071306d296feac2adb8aff07fbe5a8aa520bc82674d47a140d3a17b39", "source_text": "Let <MATH_0001> be a scheme. Let <MATH_0002> be a closed immersion\nof locally ringed spaces.\n<STRUCT_0001>\n<STRUCT_0002> The locally ringed space <MATH_0003> is a scheme,\n<STRUCT_0003> the kernel <MATH_0004> of the map\n<MATH_0005> is a quasi-coherent\nsheaf of ideals,\n<STRUCT_0004> for any affine open <MATH_0006> of <MATH_0007>\nthe morphism <MATH_0008> can be identified with\n<MATH_0009> for some ideal <MATH_0010>, and\n<STRUCT_0005> we have <MATH_0011>.\n<STRUCT_0006>\nIn particular, any sheaf of ideals locally generated by sections\nis a quasi-coherent sheaf of ideals (and vice versa),\nand any closed subspace of <MATH_0012> is a scheme.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:03183b8e147e7e1600203bdad8397dce7ebc5e82bfc73e863d3cc21282645825", "unit_id": "tag:01IN:statement"}
